/* Original educational commentary built chapter-by-chapter from the user's uploaded editions.
   Each item is a guided study, not a reproduction of the publisher's chapter.
   All examples and numbers are authored for this site. */
window.BOOK_MASTERCLASS = {
 db: [
 {n:1, pdf:31, focus:'Why a database system?', sections:['1-1 Why Databases?','1-2 Data versus Information','1-3 Introducing the Database','1-6 Problems with File System Data Processing','1-7 Database Systems'], objectives:['Explain the difference between data and decision-relevant information.','Identify file-system redundancy, program-data dependence and security limitations.','Name the components and functions of a database system.'], concepts:[
 ['From stored facts to decisions','A number such as 345.86 is a raw observation until its entity, unit, date and business meaning are supplied. The DBMS combines structured data with metadata, constraints, security and query capability. Information is derived using validated data in a relevant context.'],
 ['Why separate files become fragile','Suppose sales and billing each store an address. Editing only one produces inconsistent records. Embedding storage structure in every application makes schema changes expensive. A shared database centralizes representation and can enforce key and referential-integrity rules.'],
 ['DBMS is not just a file','The environment comprises stored data and metadata, DBMS software, hardware, people and procedures. Query processing, recovery, integrity enforcement, concurrency control, views and authorization are distinct services. A database design can still be poor even when a powerful DBMS is used.'] ],
 example:'Sales stores customer #17 at 9 Oak St; Billing stores customer #17 at 8 Oak St. Treat customer_id as the stable identity, store the current address in the CUSTOMER relation and let both workflows reference it. This removes the particular duplicate, but permissions and backup remain separate requirements.',
 exercise:'A hospital maintains laboratory, billing and admissions patient addresses in different files. Identify two anomalies, one security risk, and one data-governance requirement.', answer:'An update anomaly leaves conflicting addresses, and insertion of a patient can require creating unrelated billing data. Different file permissions risk unauthorized disclosure. A shared patient identity plus validated access policy is needed.', visual:'redundancy'},
 {n:2,pdf:61,focus:'Models and abstraction',sections:['2-1 Data Modeling and Data Models','2-3 Data Model Basic Building Blocks','2-4 Business Rules','2-6 Degrees of Data Abstraction'],objectives:['Distinguish entities, attributes, relationships and constraints.','Translate business rules into cardinality and participation requirements.','Separate external, conceptual, logical and physical perspectives.'],concepts:[
 ['Models simplify reality','An entity represents a type of thing the database must remember; an attribute describes it; a relationship expresses how instances are associated. Constraints are assertions that distinguish a valid database state from an invalid one.'],
 ['Business rules come first','“A customer may place multiple orders; each order belongs to exactly one customer” implies a one-to-many relationship and mandatory order ownership. If the actual rule allows guest orders, a NOT NULL foreign key is too restrictive. Interview domain experts rather than guessing cardinalities.'],
 ['Levels prevent accidental coupling','A user view contains the fields a user needs. The conceptual model is business-oriented. The logical design is tables, keys and data types; the physical design chooses pages, partitions and indexes. Changing the physical index need not alter a customer-facing report.']],example:'Library rule: each loan records one borrower and one copy of a book; a borrower may hold several loans. Borrower(loan_id) is incorrect. Represent BORROWER(id) 1-to-many LOAN(id, borrower_id, copy_id); distinguish a book title from a copy.',exercise:'Would “each employee works in one department” permit an employee with no assigned department? What detail is missing?',answer:'The rule does not explicitly say whether assignment is mandatory. Require explicit optionality/participation before choosing a nullable or non-nullable department_id.',visual:'erd'},
 {n:3,pdf:93,focus:'Relations, keys, integrity and algebra',sections:['3-1 A Logical View of Data','3-2 Keys','3-3 Integrity Rules','3-4 Relational Algebra','3-6 Relationships within the Relational Database'],objectives:['Differentiate superkeys, candidate keys, primary keys and foreign keys.','Predict a referential-integrity violation.','Write selection, projection and join operations.'],concepts:[
 ['A relation has rows and columns','In a relational representation, a tuple is an individual record and an attribute is a named component. Key constraints identify rows; the logical model is not ordered by default, even if a user interface renders rows in a particular sequence.'],
 ['Identity versus reference','Candidate keys are minimal unique identifiers. One candidate key is selected as primary key; others are alternate keys. A foreign key references a candidate/unique key in another table. Primary keys must not be null and foreign-key constraints protect references.'],
 ['Algebra makes queries compositional','Selection σ selects tuples satisfying a predicate. Projection π retains columns (theoretical set projection removes duplicates). Join combines related tuples. For CUSTOMER ⋈ ORDER on customer_id, INNER JOIN returns matching orders, not customers without an order.']],example:'CUSTOMER=(1,Ana),(2,Leo). ORDERS=(10,1),(11,1). A join produces two matching rows for Ana; Leo has none. A LEFT JOIN would retain Leo with missing order attributes.',exercise:'What happens if ORDERS.customer_id=99 is inserted with an enforced foreign key and no CUSTOMER 99?',answer:'The insert is rejected unless the referenced customer exists or the defined foreign key permits an appropriate null value.',visual:'joins'},
 {n:4,pdf:136,focus:'ER modeling with correct cardinality',sections:['4-1 The Entity Relationship Model','4-2 Developing an ER Diagram','4-3 Database Design Challenges: Conflicting Goals'],objectives:['Read Crow’s Foot optionality and multiplicity.','Resolve M:N relationships using associative entities.','Distinguish weak entities and identifying relationships.'],concepts:[
 ['Two questions per relationship','Cardinality asks how many occurrences are possible; participation asks whether zero is allowed. “One or many” is different from “zero or many”. Check both ends of each relationship with concrete transactions.'],
 ['An M:N relationship needs a bridge','A student enrolls in multiple sections; a section has multiple students. ENROLLMENT(student_id,section_id,grade) expresses this many-to-many relationship. The bridge may carry attributes that belong to the association, such as grade and enrollment_date.'],
 ['Draw from verified requirements','Build a list of nouns, candidate entities and business rules; identify keys; place attributes; connect entities; then validate each cardinality using counterexamples. An ERD is a hypothesis to test against the domain, not a decorative chart.']],example:'If SECTION is identified by (course_code,term,section_number), ENROLLMENT must reference the full composite key or a stable surrogate SECTION ID. A free-text course name alone does not identify a section.',exercise:'A doctor can see many patients, and a patient may see many doctors. A visit has date, room, and outcome. Where do the visit-specific fields belong?',answer:'A VISIT/ENCOUNTER associative entity linked to DOCTOR and PATIENT, rather than repeated doctor/patient fields.',visual:'erd'},
 {n:5,pdf:190,focus:'Advanced ER and specialization',sections:['5-1 The Extended Entity Relationship Model','5-2 Entity Supertypes and Subtypes','5-3 Specialization and Generalization','5-4 Entity Clusters'],objectives:['Explain subtype inheritance and why it is useful.','Specify disjoint/overlapping and total/partial constraints.','Choose a relational mapping and articulate its drawbacks.'],concepts:[
 ['Generalization reduces duplication','A PERSON supertype might store id,name,address, with STUDENT and EMPLOYEE subtypes storing specialized fields. Shared attributes need not be repeated across two independent relations.'],
 ['Specialization rules have meaning','Disjoint means an instance belongs to at most one subtype; overlapping permits several. Total specialization requires every supertype instance to be represented by at least one subtype. These are business constraints, not graphic decoration.'],
 ['Mapping choices are trade-offs','One table per hierarchy permits many nulls; a supertype table plus separate subtype tables preserves conceptual separation but requires joins; a table per concrete subtype duplicates common columns. Choose using constraints, workload and DBMS capabilities.']],example:'A university can employ a student. STUDENT and EMPLOYEE must be allowed to overlap if that rule is true; declaring them disjoint would make legitimate records impossible.',exercise:'In an animal shelter, all animals are either dogs or cats, never both. Is specialization total, partial, disjoint or overlapping?',answer:'Total and disjoint, assuming the business rule explicitly excludes every other animal type.',visual:'erd'},
 {n:6,pdf:220,focus:'Normalization, determinants and anomalies',sections:['6-1 Database Tables and Normalization','6-2 The Need for Normalization','6-3 Normalization Process','6-4 Improving the Design','6-5 Surrogate Key Considerations'],objectives:['Identify functional dependencies and candidate keys.','Derive a defensible 1NF→2NF→3NF decomposition.','Distinguish lossless joins and dependency preservation.'],concepts:[
 ['Normalize dependencies, not appearances','If (order_id,item_id) identifies a line and order_id→customer_name, customer_name depends on part of the composite key. Repeating it per line creates an update anomaly. Attributes should sit in relations determined by an appropriate key.'],
 ['1NF through 3NF','1NF requires atomic column values at the chosen logical representation. 2NF removes partial dependencies of non-prime attributes on a candidate key. 3NF restricts nontrivial dependencies X→A unless X is a superkey or A is prime. These are separate tests; merely creating smaller tables is insufficient.'],
 ['Decomposition needs proof','For R(A,B,C) with A→B and B→C, decomposition R1(A,B),R2(B,C) is lossless because B is a key of R2 in this example. Check whether important dependencies can be enforced locally after decomposition.'],
 ['Denormalization is deliberate','A denormalized reporting cache may improve a measured workload, but materialized repetitions require an explicit refresh and consistency strategy. First establish a correct logical model.']],example:'ORDER_LINE(order_id,product_id,quantity,customer_name) has key (order_id,product_id), but order_id→customer_name. Split ORDERS(order_id,customer_id), ORDER_LINE(order_id,product_id,quantity), CUSTOMER(customer_id,name), provided these dependencies hold.',exercise:'Which dependency violates 2NF when key=(student_id,course_id): student_id→student_name or (student_id,course_id)→grade?',answer:'student_id→student_name is a partial dependency of a non-prime attribute on a proper part of the composite key.',visual:'normalization'},
 {n:7,pdf:267,focus:'SQL retrieval and joins',sections:['7-1 SQL Basics','7-2 SQL Data Definition Commands','7-3 SQL Data Manipulation Commands','7-4 Advanced Data Definition Commands'],objectives:['Write SQL filters and joins with correct semantics.','Understand grouping before filtering with HAVING.','Distinguish data definition and data manipulation.'],concepts:[
 ['A query is a declarative request','SELECT lists output expressions, FROM establishes data sources, WHERE filters candidate rows, GROUP BY forms groups, HAVING filters groups, ORDER BY sorts displayed results. Conceptual SQL evaluation order is not the literal clause order.'],
 ['Joins can multiply results','Joining each customer to its orders produces one result per qualifying matching order, not necessarily one result per customer. Always specify join predicates; a missing predicate can cause a Cartesian product.'],
 ['Aggregates have distinct semantics','COUNT(*) counts rows, COUNT(column) ignores NULLs, AVG(column) ignores NULL values. WHERE cannot directly filter on grouped COUNT; use HAVING after GROUP BY.']],example:'SELECT c.CUS_CODE, COUNT(i.INV_NUMBER) AS n FROM CUSTOMER c LEFT JOIN INVOICE i ON c.CUS_CODE=i.CUS_CODE GROUP BY c.CUS_CODE; returns a row for every customer, including those with zero invoices.',exercise:'Why does SELECT vendor_id, COUNT(*) FROM products WHERE COUNT(*)>3 GROUP BY vendor_id fail?',answer:'The aggregate predicate belongs in HAVING COUNT(*)>3, after GROUP BY; WHERE filters rows before groups exist.',visual:'joins'},
 {n:8,pdf:379,focus:'SQL implementation features',sections:['8-1 SQL Data Definition Commands','8-2 SQL Data Manipulation Commands','8-3 Virtual Tables: Creating a View','8-4 Creating and Using Indexes','8-5 Procedural SQL'],objectives:['Use views with the correct security limitations.','Explain triggers and stored procedures and their side effects.','Compare correlated and uncorrelated subqueries.'],concepts:[
 ['Views are named queries','A view provides a reusable relational interface and can hide columns from authorized consumers, subject to privilege configuration. Views are not automatically materialized or indexed in every DBMS.'],
 ['Procedural database logic','A trigger runs in response to a specified table event. A procedure encapsulates steps. Recursion, unintended cascades and hidden side effects complicate debugging; prefer explicit invariants and tests.'],
 ['Correlated queries depend on a row','EXISTS may test whether a matching record exists for each outer row. A correlated subquery refers to a value from its enclosing query. Query optimizers may rewrite these expressions; do not assume a particular nested-loop execution order.']],example:'SELECT c.CUS_CODE FROM CUSTOMER c WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM INVOICE i WHERE i.CUS_CODE=c.CUS_CODE) returns customers with at least one invoice; unlike an unqualified join it cannot duplicate the customer solely due to invoice multiplicity.',exercise:'Should a trigger silently send an email after inserting an order? Identify one failure mode.',answer:'External email delivery may not share the database transaction; rollback or retries can produce duplicate or contradictory notifications. Use a durable outbox with idempotent processing.',visual:'joins'},
 {n:9,pdf:459,focus:'Conceptual, logical and physical design',sections:['9-1 Database Design within the Information Systems Life Cycle','9-2 The Database Life Cycle','9-3 Conceptual Design','9-4 DBMS Software Selection','9-5 Logical Design','9-6 Physical Design'],objectives:['Map requirements to an ER model and logical schema.','Choose integrity constraints and storage structures.','Validate a design against workload and realistic user needs.'],concepts:[
 ['Requirements are observable','Record data owners, queries, transaction volumes, consistency requirements and rules. Design that “looks normalized” may still fail if a legitimate business process cannot be represented.'],
 ['Iterative stages','Conceptual design captures domain entities/rules; logical design maps to tables, keys and constraints; physical design chooses indexing, partitioning and access paths. DBMS selection affects achievable implementations.'],
 ['Use a testable design review','For every important workflow, trace inserts, updates, deletes and joins through the model. Verify constraints, permissions, transactions, backup needs and relevant performance targets.']],example:'A university course-registration design must reject a section enrollment once its capacity is reached. Neither a foreign key nor a simple uniqueness constraint alone enforces the capacity rule; concurrency-safe transaction logic is also needed.',exercise:'Identify conceptual, logical and physical choices for a hospital appointment system.',answer:'Conceptual: patient–appointment–provider business relationships. Logical: foreign keys and unique scheduling constraints. Physical: an index on provider_id and appointment_time with performance tests.',visual:'erd'},
 {n:10,pdf:501,focus:'ACID, anomalies and schedules',sections:['10-1 Transaction Management','10-2 Concurrency Control','10-3 Concurrency Control with Locking Methods','10-4 Concurrency Control with Time Stamping Methods','10-5 Concurrency Control with Optimistic Methods','10-6 Database Recovery Management'],objectives:['Diagnose lost updates and dirty reads.','Understand lock compatibility and isolation.','Explain why recovery and atomicity are separate from locking.'],concepts:[
 ['A transaction defines an all-or-nothing unit','Atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability (ACID) describe different requirements. A transaction that transfers money needs both account updates to commit or neither; application invariants define what consistency means.'],
 ['Interleaving causes anomalies','If two transactions read balance=100 and independently write 110 and 120, a lost update can occur. A schedule is an ordering of their read/write actions; serializability characterizes when interleaving is equivalent to some serial order.'],
 ['Locks have compatibility rules','Shared/read locks can coexist under a conventional lock model; exclusive/write locks conflict with other readers/writers. Two-phase locking can support serializability, but its variants, deadlocks and visibility semantics differ by DBMS.'],
 ['Recovery is not the same as isolation','Logging and undo/redo mechanisms support crash recovery. Isolation coordinates concurrent observers; durability concerns committed outcomes surviving relevant failures.']],example:'T1 reads stock 10 and plans stock 9; T2 reads stock 10 and plans stock 8. Writing 9 then 8 loses T1’s decrement. Atomic UPDATE stock=stock−q with appropriate concurrency controls avoids this specific read-modify-write race.',exercise:'T1 writes an uncommitted price change; T2 reads it; T1 rolls back. Name the anomaly.',answer:'Dirty read (uncommitted dependency). A suitable isolation mechanism prevents visibility of uncommitted writes.',visual:'schedule'},
 {n:11,pdf:534,focus:'Query plans, indexes and measurement',sections:['11-1 Database Performance Tuning','11-2 Query Processing','11-3 Indexes and Query Optimization','11-4 Query Optimizer','11-5 SQL Performance Tuning'],objectives:['Contrast access cost of scans and selective indexes.','Interpret an EXPLAIN plan cautiously.','Measure before-and-after execution with equivalent datasets.'],concepts:[
 ['Performance is a system property','Response time depends on client, network, DBMS, CPU, memory, storage and contention. An SQL rewrite that speeds one query may harm write throughput or caching.'],
 ['Query optimizer considers alternatives','Parsing, optimization and execution are distinguishable steps. Statistics, cardinality estimates, join orders and index selectivity guide a cost-based optimizer; “uses index” does not always mean “faster”.'],
 ['Indexes trade reads for writes','A B-tree index can locate selective predicates efficiently but consumes space and maintenance work. A full scan may be cheaper when most rows match or the table is tiny.'],
 ['Measure paired workloads','Control database size, cache state, concurrency and query result equivalence. Report p50 and tail latency rather than only the fastest isolated run.']],example:'For a 1,000,000-row table, searching one indexed unique key generally avoids reading most heap pages; reading 900,000 rows may instead favor a sequential scan. The break-even point depends on physical layout and hardware.',exercise:'An index on status (two possible values) makes a report that returns 80% of rows slower. Is the optimizer necessarily defective?',answer:'No. Poor selectivity and random table lookups can cost more than scanning sequentially.',visual:'index'},
 {n:12,pdf:567,focus:'Distributed data placement and consistency',sections:['12-1 Distributed Database Management Systems','12-2 DDBMS Components','12-3 Distributed Database Transparency Features','12-4 Distributed Database Design','12-5 Database Transaction Characteristics','12-6 Distributed Database Performance and Recovery'],objectives:['Describe fragmentation, allocation and replication separately.','Explain transparency and distributed transactions.','Identify availability and failure handling risks.'],concepts:[
 ['Fragmentation is not replication','Horizontal fragmentation partitions rows; vertical fragmentation partitions attributes while retaining reconstruction keys. Replication creates copies to improve read locality or resilience, and introduces synchronization questions.'],
 ['One logical database, many sites','Location transparency reduces client exposure to placement decisions. But latency, network partitions and node failures cannot be hidden without performance or availability consequences.'],
 ['Distributed commits are costly','Coordinating atomic transactions across participants can require protocols such as two-phase commit, with blocking and failure-recovery concerns. Do not confuse two-phase commit with two-phase locking.']],example:'A retailer partitions orders by region but replicates its product catalog. Regional order writes remain local; catalog updates require a deliberate consistency strategy and failover assumptions.',exercise:'Does copying a table to two servers guarantee high availability?',answer:'No. Shared failure modes, network partitions, coordination and stale replicas can prevent correct service even with copies.',visual:'distribution'},
 {n:13,pdf:601,focus:'Warehouses, OLAP and star schemas',sections:['13-1 The Need for Data Analysis','13-2 Business Intelligence','13-3 Decision Support Data','13-4 The Data Warehouse','13-5 Star Schemas','13-6 Online Analytical Processing','13-9 Data Visualization'],objectives:['Distinguish OLTP workloads from analytics workloads.','Design fact and dimension tables at a declared grain.','Avoid double-counting after joining dimensions.'],concepts:[
 ['Grain determines the meaning of a fact','A sales fact table might contain one row per order line, not one per order. Its grain determines valid aggregations. Dimensions such as date, product and store describe the fact’s coordinates.'],
 ['Warehouses favor analysis','Transaction systems optimize operational correctness and short writes; analytic systems support large scans, historical trends and consistent business metrics. ETL/ELT integration introduces source quality, late-arriving records and lineage issues.'],
 ['OLAP operations have precise meaning','Slice restricts one dimension; dice restricts several; roll-up aggregates to a coarser level; drill-down reveals finer detail. A count of rows is not necessarily a count of customers.']],example:'FactSales(date_id,product_id,store_id,quantity,revenue) has grain one product–store–day. Joining two many-to-many dimensions without bridging may multiply revenue; confirm join cardinalities.',exercise:'A chart compares revenue across countries and months. Which operation aggregates daily facts into months?',answer:'Roll-up along the time hierarchy, with an explicit time/calendar mapping.',visual:'star'},
 {n:14,pdf:668,focus:'Big data, NoSQL and data models',sections:['14-1 Big Data','14-2 Hadoop','14-3 NoSQL','14-4 NewSQL Databases','14-5 Working with Document Databases Using MongoDB','14-6 Working with Graph Databases Using Neo4j'],objectives:['Compare document, key–value, wide-column and graph models.','Identify access-pattern-driven schema design.','Reject the claim that NoSQL always means no transactions.'],concepts:[
 ['Choose a model for the workload','Documents serve aggregates with nested structures; key–value stores support key-based access; graph databases are natural for multi-hop relationships. A relational design may still be best when joins, constraints and transactions dominate.'],
 ['Distributed systems are not free','Horizontal scalability introduces partitions, distributed coordination and operational complexity. Statements such as “NoSQL has no schema” are misleading: the schema may live in application code or validation rules.'],
 ['Big data is more than size','Velocity, variety, quality and the cost of moving data shape architectures. Batch jobs and streams use different latency assumptions; not every small application needs a distributed computation platform.']],example:'Store a product catalog as JSON documents when product families have heterogeneous optional attributes, while retaining an independent relational order ledger for financial integrity if those transaction semantics are crucial.',exercise:'A recommendation query needs 4-hop traversals through a social graph. Which database characteristic matters more than simply counting documents?',answer:'Efficient graph traversal and relationship representation; benchmark query patterns and update constraints.',visual:'distribution'},
 {n:15,pdf:703,focus:'Connecting apps safely to databases',sections:['15-1 Database Connectivity','15-2 Database Internet Connectivity','15-3 Extensible Markup Language (XML)','15-4 Cloud Computing Services'],objectives:['Separate application, API and database responsibility.','Explain connection pooling and parameterized SQL.','Recognize cloud shared-responsibility requirements.'],concepts:[
 ['Connectivity is an architectural boundary','Applications use drivers, APIs and network protocols to access data. A browser should normally call a limited server-side API rather than directly holding privileged database credentials.'],
 ['Parameterization prevents SQL injection','Parameters bind user values without concatenating them into executable SQL structure. Escaping strings by hand is an error-prone substitute. Privileges must also restrict what an attacker can do if an endpoint fails.'],
 ['Cloud does not outsource governance','Managed services may handle some patching, backups or infrastructure, but data classification, schema correctness, credentials, network access, recovery tests and regional requirements remain customer responsibilities.']],example:'SELECT * FROM customer WHERE email = ? with an independently bound parameter is safer than adding user input to a SQL string. Rate-limit and authorize the surrounding endpoint as well.',exercise:'Should a public webpage embed a database administrator password in JavaScript?',answer:'No. Client-side code is inspectable; use a server-mediated API and least-privilege database credentials.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:16,pdf:743,focus:'Database governance and security',sections:['16-1 Data as a Corporate Asset','16-2 The Need for a Database and Its Role in an Organization','16-6 Security','16-7 Database Administration Tools','16-8 Developing a Data Administration Strategy','16-9 The DBA’s Role in the Cloud'],objectives:['Distinguish authorization, integrity, backup and audit.','Create least-privilege roles and recovery objectives.','Define concrete governance responsibilities.'],concepts:[
 ['People and process matter','Data governance establishes ownership, quality definitions, stewardship, retention and access rules. A technical security control cannot resolve an ambiguous data-owner policy on its own.'],
 ['Defense in depth','Use least-privilege roles, network boundaries, encryption, audit logging, patching and secure application design. Encrypting disk storage does not by itself prevent authorized-account misuse or SQL injection.'],
 ['Recovery must be tested','RPO constrains acceptable data loss; RTO constrains acceptable restoration time. A backup that cannot be restored or that cannot meet the service objective is insufficient.']],example:'For a transactional service with RPO=15 minutes and RTO=2 hours, the backup/archive and failover process must be demonstrated to restore data no older than 15 minutes and service within two hours under the defined failure.',exercise:'A DBA has backups but has never run a restore drill. What remains unverified?',answer:'Whether backup files are complete, restorable, timely and compatible with the target recovery environment.',visual:'pipeline'}
 ],
 rl: [
 {n:1,pdf:23,focus:'Agent–environment interaction',sections:['1.1 Reinforcement Learning','1.3 Elements of Reinforcement Learning','1.5 An Extended Example: Tic-Tac-Toe'],objectives:['Distinguish policy, reward signal, value function and model.','Explain why exploration is necessary.'],concepts:[['Learning from consequences','At time t, an agent observes state S_t, selects A_t using a policy, and receives reward R_(t+1) and a new state S_(t+1). Long-term return, not immediate reward alone, defines the control objective.'],['Four different components','A policy maps states to actions; a reward signal expresses immediate feedback; a value function estimates expected future return; an optional environment model predicts transitions or rewards. A learning system may not include all four as separate learned modules.']],example:'In tic-tac-toe a move with no immediate reward can still be best because it changes the probability of winning later.',exercise:'Why might an agent try a poorly estimated action?',answer:'To gather information that may reveal a higher-value action; this is exploration.',visual:'mdp'},
 {n:2,pdf:47,focus:'Bandits: exploration, exploitation, regret',sections:['2.1 A k-armed Bandit Problem','2.2 Action-value Methods','2.4 Incremental Implementation','2.7 Upper-Confidence-Bound Action Selection'],objectives:['Compute incremental action-value estimates.','Compare epsilon-greedy and UCB exploration.'],concepts:[['The bandit setting','Each chosen arm gives a stochastic reward but no state-transition decision. The central trade-off is to exploit arms with high estimates or explore uncertain ones.'],['Sample-average estimate','For a stationary arm with n rewards, Q_n is the mean observed reward. Update it incrementally: Q_(n+1)=Q_n + (1/n)(R_n−Q_n). Constant stepsizes emphasize recent observations in nonstationary environments.'],['Do not confuse the evaluation axes','Average reward measures realized performance; the fraction of optimal-arm selections measures identification of the best arm in a controlled simulation. Regret depends on the reward gap and horizon.']],example:'An arm has rewards 1,0,1. After three pulls its sample-average value is 2/3; if a fourth reward is 0, the new value is 1/2.',exercise:'For epsilon=0.1 and 4 arms, what is the probability of choosing a specified greedy arm under uniform random exploration?',answer:'0.9+0.1/4=0.925, assuming a unique greedy arm and uniform exploration.',visual:'bandit'},
 {n:3,pdf:69,focus:'Finite Markov decision processes',sections:['3.1 The Agent–Environment Interface','3.3 Returns and Episodes','3.5 Policies and Value Functions','3.7 Optimal Value Functions'],objectives:['Compute discounted returns.','Recognize Bellman expectation and optimality equations.'],concepts:[['Markov property','A state representation is Markov when the next-state/reward distribution given present state and action does not need earlier history. State design matters; raw observations may be partially observable.'],['Discounted return','G_t=R_(t+1)+γR_(t+2)+γ²R_(t+3)+... . A discount below one prioritizes nearer rewards and aids convergence in continuing settings.'],['Bellman decomposition','vπ(s)=Σ_a π(a|s) Σ_(s′,r) p(s′,r|s,a)[r+γvπ(s′)]. The equation recurses via the successor-state value.']],example:'For rewards [1,2,3] and γ=0.5, the three-step discounted return is 1+0.5·2+0.25·3=2.75.',exercise:'If γ=0 and rewards are immediate, what does state value represent?',answer:'Expected immediate reward under the policy, apart from terminal and indexing conventions.',visual:'mdp'},
 {n:4,pdf:95,focus:'Dynamic programming backups',sections:['4.1 Policy Evaluation (Prediction)','4.2 Policy Improvement','4.3 Policy Iteration','4.4 Value Iteration'],objectives:['Trace synchronous Bellman backups.','Distinguish evaluation, improvement and value iteration.'],concepts:[['DP assumes the dynamics are available','Tabular planning can compute expected backups using p(s′,r|s,a), unlike experience-based model-free updates.'],['Policy iteration','Alternates estimating values under the current policy with choosing actions that improve expected return. Value iteration folds a greedy backup into each value update.'],['Stopping rules are approximate','In practice terminate when successive value changes are small, but report tolerance and assumptions. A small difference does not have universal meaning without discount and reward bounds.']],example:'If action A yields certain reward 1 and terminal state, and B yields certain reward 2 and terminal state, a one-step optimal backup assigns value 2 to the starting state.',exercise:'Why is policy iteration not simply repeatedly taking the highest observed reward?',answer:'It uses expected future returns under a model and evaluates/improves a policy over states.',visual:'mdp'},
 {n:5,pdf:113,focus:'Monte Carlo estimates from complete episodes',sections:['5.1 Monte Carlo Prediction','5.3 Monte Carlo Control','5.4 Monte Carlo Control without Exploring Starts'],objectives:['Calculate first-visit empirical returns.','Explain why episode completion is needed for basic MC prediction.'],concepts:[['Returns rather than transitions','MC estimates vπ(s) from complete sampled returns following visits to s. It does not require a known transition model but standard episodic formulations wait for the episode to finish.'],['First versus every visit','First-visit MC uses the return following the first occurrence of a state within each episode; every-visit MC uses all visits. Their sampling dependence differs.'],['Off-policy requires careful weights','Importance sampling compensates for differences between behavior and target policies; large ratios may induce high variance.']],example:'In two episodes the returns from a target state are 3 and 7; the first-visit MC mean is (3+7)/2=5. In a long episode an every-visit estimate may count multiple returns.',exercise:'Can textbook basic MC prediction update a state value before the episode has terminated?',answer:'Not with a complete-return target, because the return is not yet available.',visual:'mc'},
 {n:6,pdf:141,focus:'Temporal-difference updates',sections:['6.1 TD Prediction','6.2 Advantages of TD Prediction Methods','6.4 Sarsa: On-policy TD Control','6.5 Q-learning: Off-policy TD Control'],objectives:['Calculate a TD(0) update.','Compare SARSA and Q-learning targets.'],concepts:[['Bootstrap from an estimate','TD(0) target r+γV(s′) mixes sampled reward with a learned continuation estimate. The difference from V(s) is the TD error δ.'],['SARSA is on-policy','Its action-value target is r+γQ(s′,a′), where a′ is the next action actually chosen by the behavior policy. It learns about that policy’s future action choices.'],['Q-learning is off-policy','Its target is r+γmax_a Q(s′,a) regardless of which next action was sampled. In the tabular setting, the usual convergence conditions are important.']],example:'V(s)=2, r=1, V(s′)=4, γ=0.9, α=0.3: target=4.6, error=2.6, new V(s)=2.78.',exercise:'Which target changes when exploration changes the chosen next action: SARSA or Q-learning?',answer:'SARSA uses the sampled next action; Q-learning uses the maximum target independent of that sampled action.',visual:'td'},
 {n:7,pdf:163,focus:'n-step bootstrapping',sections:['7.1 n-step TD Prediction','7.2 n-step Sarsa','7.3 n-step Off-policy Learning'],objectives:['Construct a truncated n-step target.','Describe bias/variance effects of backup horizon.'],concepts:[['An intermediate target','n-step TD uses n observed rewards plus a discounted bootstrap: G^(n)_t=∑_(k=1)^n γ^(k−1)R_(t+k)+γ^n V(S_(t+n)).'],['The backup length changes information','One-step TD learns quickly but bootstraps heavily; complete-return MC uses much more sampled outcome information. n-step methods form a family between them.']],example:'For n=2, r1=1, r2=2, γ=0.5 and V(s2)=4, the target is 1+0.5·2+0.25·4=3.',exercise:'If an episode ends before the n-th step, do you bootstrap from an imagined nonterminal state?',answer:'No; truncate at termination, using the terminal convention.',visual:'td'},
 {n:8,pdf:181,focus:'Planning and model learning',sections:['8.1 Models and Planning','8.2 Dyna: Integrated Planning, Acting, and Learning','8.3 When the Model Is Wrong'],objectives:['Describe real versus simulated transitions.','Explain how model errors can bias planning.'],concepts:[['Dyna combines sources of updates','Experience can update value estimates directly and also update a learned model; simulated model transitions support extra planning updates.'],['Imperfect models need monitoring','A model learned before the environment changes can keep generating misleading simulated experience; prioritized sweeping and exploration mechanisms address different planning challenges.']],example:'After a corridor becomes blocked, a planner using an old transition model may repeatedly choose the blocked action until real experience repairs that model.',exercise:'Why do five extra simulated updates not necessarily equal five real observations?',answer:'Simulated updates are conditioned on model accuracy; they do not add independent environmental evidence.',visual:'mdp'}
 ],
 d2l: [
 {n:1,pdf:43,focus:'The learning problem and its ingredients',sections:['1.1 A Motivating Example','1.2 Key Components','1.3 Kinds of Machine Learning Problems'],objectives:['Separate data, model, objective and optimization.','Classify supervised and reinforcement-learning tasks.'],concepts:[['Learning is an empirical process','A dataset expresses a sample of observations; a model class restricts the predictions a learner can express; a loss/objective evaluates predictions; an optimization algorithm chooses parameters.'],['Data-generating context matters','A low empirical loss can hide leakage, skew or distribution shift. Define held-out evaluation and provenance at the start of a project.']],example:'Predicting house sale price is a regression problem; predicting whether an image contains a dog is classification. Both require evaluated generalization, not memorization.',exercise:'Which element does stochastic gradient descent belong to: model, objective or optimization?',answer:'Optimization.',visual:'gradient'},
 {n:2,pdf:72,focus:'Preliminaries, tensors and autodiff',sections:['2.1 Data Manipulation','2.2 Data Preprocessing','2.3 Linear Algebra','2.4 Calculus','2.5 Automatic Differentiation'],objectives:['Interpret tensor shapes and broadcasting.','Apply chain rule and automatic differentiation.'],concepts:[['Shape is part of semantics','A batch×features matrix and a features vector can combine via broadcasting; always check alignment, accidental expansion and memory cost.'],['Reverse-mode differentiation','Modern frameworks build a computation graph from operations and propagate derivatives using the chain rule. Detaching a tensor cuts gradient propagation.']],example:'For y=(wx+b)², dy/dw=2(wx+b)x; w=2,x=3,b=1 gives 42. This hand derivative is what autodiff should reproduce.',exercise:'Why does converting intermediate tensor values to plain Python floats often break gradient tracking?',answer:'The resulting scalar is outside the original differentiation graph.',visual:'gradient'},
 {n:3,pdf:124,focus:'Linear regression and generalization',sections:['3.1 Linear Regression','3.4 Linear Regression Implementation from Scratch','3.6 Generalization','3.7 Weight Decay'],objectives:['Derive mean squared error gradients.','Distinguish generalization from training loss.'],concepts:[['The linear model','For prediction ŷ=wᵀx+b, squared error penalizes squared residuals. Mini-batch SGD approximates the gradient over samples.'],['Weight decay','An L2 penalty encourages smaller weights and changes the optimum; it is not automatically a cure for leakage or mislabeling.']],example:'If ŷ=wx, target y=4 and x=2, with L=(ŷ−y)²/2, then at w=1 the gradient (wx−y)x=−4. With η=0.1 the updated w=1.4.',exercise:'What happens to the training loss if you add a regularization penalty but still compare only unpenalized prediction error?',answer:'The optimization target changes, so lower unpenalized training MSE is not guaranteed.',visual:'gradient'},
 {n:4,pdf:169,focus:'Classification, softmax and distribution shift',sections:['4.1 Softmax Regression','4.5 Generalization in Classification','4.6 Environment and Distribution Shift'],objectives:['Interpret softmax scores as normalized probabilities.','Explain cross-entropy and label shift.'],concepts:[['Scores are not probabilities','Softmax converts logits z to exp(z_i)/∑_j exp(z_j). Numerically stable implementations subtract max(logit) before exponentiation.'],['Cross-entropy penalizes confident errors','For the true class y, L=−log p_y. A confident incorrect prediction can incur a very large loss. Calibration and classification accuracy answer different questions.']],example:'For logits [0,ln 2], probabilities are [1/3,2/3]. If the first class is true, cross-entropy is −ln(1/3)≈1.099.',exercise:'What is the effect of adding the same constant to every softmax logit?',answer:'No change to probabilities, because common factors cancel.',visual:'softmax'},
 {n:5,pdf:211,focus:'MLPs, activation and backpropagation',sections:['5.1 Multilayer Perceptrons','5.3 Forward Propagation, Backward Propagation, and Computational Graphs','5.4 Numerical Stability and Initialization','5.6 Dropout'],objectives:['Trace one forward pass and its gradients.','Explain why nonlinearities are needed.'],concepts:[['Stacking linear maps alone is still linear','Without nonlinear activations, composition of affine layers can be represented as one affine transformation. ReLU, tanh and other nonlinearities expand representational capacity.'],['Backpropagation is chain rule at scale','Store/recompute needed activations, propagate output-loss derivatives backward through graph nodes, then update trainable parameters.'],['Dropout is not test-time noise by default','Common inverted dropout scales retained activations during training; during inference the model uses the complete network without random dropping.']],example:'A hidden ReLU neuron has preactivation z=−2: its output is 0 and standard ReLU derivative is 0 there. At z=3 its output is 3 and derivative is 1.',exercise:'Can multiplying two ordinary linear layers create XOR without nonlinear activation?',answer:'No. Their composition remains a linear/affine mapping.',visual:'network'},
 {n:6,pdf:254,focus:'Modules, parameters and GPUs',sections:['6.1 Layers and Modules','6.2 Parameter Management','6.3 Parameter Initialization','6.4 Lazy Initialization','6.7 GPUs'],objectives:['Differentiate parameters from activations.','Explain device placement and memory limitations.'],concepts:[['Reusable modules','A framework module groups parameterized operations and submodules, enabling consistent initialization, saving, loading and moving tensors across devices.'],['Reproducibility requires more than a seed','Set library seeds, track versions and hardware, check nondeterministic kernels, and save the exact training configuration and checkpoint.']],example:'A dense layer mapping 10 input features to 4 output units has 10×4=40 weights plus 4 bias terms: 44 trainable scalar parameters.',exercise:'Why does moving only model weights to a GPU while leaving the input batch on CPU fail?',answer:'Tensor operations generally require compatible device placement; transfer the input to the model device.',visual:'network'}
 ]
};

// Source-oriented systems guides: original worked explanations, not textbook reproduction.
Object.assign(window.BOOK_MASTERCLASS,{
 os:[
 {n:1,pdf:25,focus:'What the operating system controls',objectives:['Separate OS resource management from application code.','Explain interrupts, dual-mode execution and privileged operations.'],concepts:[['OS as a manager','The operating system mediates CPU, memory, storage and device access while exposing abstractions such as processes, files and address spaces. The user-facing program does not need to schedule disk commands or directly manage page frames.'],['Interrupts and kernel mode','Hardware interrupts, exceptions and system calls transfer control to a designated kernel handler. Privileged instructions and memory protection prevent ordinary user code from bypassing the OS policy.']],example:'An editor saves a file using a system-call interface. The kernel validates permissions, selects a file-system driver, manages buffers, and reports errors. A user process cannot safely assume it owns the storage controller.',exercise:'Why can an application not directly disable system-wide interrupts?',answer:'Disabling interrupts is privileged because it can disrupt scheduling, input/output and isolation for the entire system.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:2,pdf:77,focus:'System calls, interfaces and kernel structure',objectives:['Describe the user–kernel boundary.','Compare monolithic, layered, modular and microkernel trade-offs.'],concepts:[['System calls are a controlled boundary','A library wrapper may provide a convenient API, but the kernel validates system-call arguments and privileges. Parameter passing uses registers, blocks or stacks depending on architecture and ABI.'],['OS structure shapes failure isolation','Monolithic kernels call many services in kernel space; microkernel designs place more services in processes; modules allow a kernel to extend functionality. There is no unconditional winner: performance, failure containment and engineering complexity trade off.']],example:'A user program calls read(fd,buffer,n). The kernel checks whether fd is open and readable, validates writable user memory, and either returns data, blocks, or reports an error.',exercise:'Does moving a file server to user space automatically improve performance?',answer:'Not necessarily. IPC and context transitions add overhead, although isolation and maintainability may improve.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:3,pdf:127,focus:'Processes, states and IPC',objectives:['Read process state transitions.','Compare shared memory and message-passing IPC.'],concepts:[['Process is an execution context','A process has an address space, open resources and state described by a process control block. Its states include ready, running, waiting and terminated; scheduling transitions move among them.'],['IPC choices change coordination','Shared memory is efficient for exchanging data but needs synchronization; message passing simplifies some ownership boundaries but involves communication overhead and protocol decisions.']],example:'A process blocks while reading from an empty pipe. It is not “using the CPU slowly”; it has moved to a waiting state and another ready process can run.',exercise:'How do ready and blocked states differ?',answer:'Ready has all resources except CPU time; blocked waits for an event/resource and is not runnable even if the CPU is idle.',visual:'os-schedule'},
 {n:4,pdf:185,focus:'Threads and multicore execution',objectives:['Distinguish threads from processes.','Identify shared and private thread state.'],concepts:[['Threads share an address space','Threads in one process share memory and many resources but have separate program counters, registers and stacks. This allows lightweight communication while increasing race-condition exposure.'],['Parallelism is not concurrency','Concurrency is overlapping progress; parallelism is simultaneous execution on different cores. Multiple threads can be concurrent on one core without executing simultaneously.']],example:'Two threads increment a shared counter. The read-modify-write sequence can lose increments without an atomic primitive or lock even though each line of source code looks simple.',exercise:'Do two threads in the same process normally have independent heap memory?',answer:'No. They share the process heap/address space unless data are deliberately isolated or privately allocated.',visual:'os-schedule'},
 {n:5,pdf:225,focus:'Critical sections and synchronization',objectives:['State mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting.','Explain mutex versus counting semaphore.'],concepts:[['Critical-section requirements','Mutual exclusion prevents overlapping access to protected shared state. Progress and bounded waiting constrain whether a process can eventually enter. Atomicity of primitive operations is essential.'],['A semaphore tracks permits','wait(P) decrements/acquires a permit; signal(V) releases one. A counting semaphore can manage finite resources while a mutex expresses exclusive ownership. Busy waiting and starvation depend on implementation and policy.']],example:'For buffer capacity 3, initialize semaphore empty=3 and full=0. A producer must first acquire empty, insert item, then signal full; a consumer does the reverse.',exercise:'Can a mutex alone ensure an empty bounded buffer never overflows?',answer:'Not by itself; mutual exclusion protects updates, while resource-count coordination is also needed.',visual:'os-schedule'},
 {n:6,pdf:291,focus:'CPU scheduling and response time',objectives:['Compute waiting and turnaround times.','Distinguish FCFS, SJF, preemption and round-robin.'],concepts:[['Schedule metrics','Waiting time measures time queued for CPU; turnaround = completion−arrival; response = first CPU execution−arrival. Metrics differ when I/O or preemption is present.'],['Shortest burst is not always fair','For simultaneous jobs of lengths 6,2,3, FCFS waiting is 0,6,8 (mean 4.67), and SJF waiting is 0,2,5 (mean 2.33). The calculation assumes burst lengths are known and zero switching cost.'],['Time slicing changes responsiveness','Round-robin uses a quantum to bound how long a ready process waits between turns when the queue is finite, at the cost of extra context switches.']],example:'Under FCFS, P1=6,P2=2,P3=3 complete at times 6,8,11. With arrival time zero, turnaround times are those completion times. Response times are 0,6,8.',exercise:'Why may a very small round-robin quantum decrease throughput?',answer:'Frequent context switches consume CPU time rather than performing useful process work.',visual:'os-schedule'},
 {n:7,pdf:347,focus:'Address translation and paging',objectives:['Separate logical from physical addresses.','Compute page number and offset.'],concepts:[['Paging maps virtual pages to frames','An address space is divided into equal-sized virtual pages; physical RAM is divided into frames. Page tables map allocated pages to frames, enabling noncontiguous allocation.'],['TLBs cache address translations','A translation lookaside buffer stores recently used page-table mappings. A TLB miss need not be a page fault: the mapping may be valid but not cached.']],example:'With 4096-byte pages, virtual address 12293 has page number floor(12293/4096)=3 and offset 5. A page table finds the physical frame used to construct the physical address.',exercise:'Is every TLB miss a disk access?',answer:'No. Page-table information can be resident in RAM; only a missing/nonresident memory page may require disk I/O.',visual:'memory'},
 {n:8,pdf:393,focus:'Virtual memory and page replacement',objectives:['Explain demand paging and page faults.','Trace FIFO versus LRU page replacement.'],concepts:[['Virtual memory extends an abstraction','Demand paging loads a page when accessed. The fault handler validates the address, obtains an available frame or selects a victim, updates translation and resumes the instruction when possible.'],['Replacement policy impacts fault count','FIFO evicts the oldest-loaded page; LRU evicts the least-recently-used page. Neither is identical to the theoretical optimal offline replacement algorithm. The reference string and number of frames determine fault counts.'],['Thrashing is a working-set problem','When active working sets exceed available physical frames, frequent faults consume substantial time. Adding multiprogramming can lower useful throughput in that regime.']],example:'For 3 frames and reference sequence 1,2,3,1,4, LRU evicts page 2 when 4 arrives because page 1 was recently used. FIFO evicts page 1, its oldest loaded page.',exercise:'Why can FIFO have more faults after adding a frame for some reference strings?',answer:'FIFO can exhibit Belady’s anomaly; its frame sets are not required to have the stack inclusion property.',visual:'memory'},
 {n:9,pdf:463,focus:'Secondary-storage structures and scheduling',objectives:['Identify seek, rotation and transfer components in HDD latency.','Distinguish disk scheduling from flash/SSD behavior.'],concepts:[['Storage is a hierarchy','Access cost includes device characteristics, controller behavior and caching. HDDs have mechanical seek/rotational terms, whereas SSDs have different latency, erase and write-amplification issues.'],['Scheduling balances movement and fairness','For HDD request queues, SSTF can reduce movement but may starve distant requests; SCAN sweeps the head along tracks. Their performance depends on workload and start position.']],example:'Head at cylinder 50 with requests [10,55,95]. SSTF serves 55 first (5 cylinders), then 95 (40), then 10 (85), total 130 cylinder movements.',exercise:'Why should HDD seek-time formulas not be applied directly to SSDs?',answer:'SSDs have no moving read/write head; latency is dominated by electronic and flash-management mechanisms.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:10,pdf:499,focus:'Files, directories and access rights',objectives:['Distinguish file metadata from file content.','Explain file allocation and directory namespaces.'],concepts:[['A file is a named abstraction','The OS exposes operations such as open, read, write and close. Metadata includes size, timestamps, ownership and permission bits, while directories map names to filesystem objects.'],['Names differ from content identity','Hard links can provide multiple directory entries to one inode-like object; symbolic links carry a pathname reference. Deletion and traversal semantics therefore differ.']],example:'Removing one of two hard links does not necessarily delete the underlying data; the filesystem frees it when no references remain and no process requires it.',exercise:'Why does a symbolic link sometimes break after moving its target?',answer:'Because the stored pathname may no longer resolve, unlike a hard link to the same filesystem object.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:11,pdf:539,focus:'File-system internals and crash consistency',objectives:['Explain inode-like metadata and free-space tracking.','Describe consistency problems after interrupted writes.'],concepts:[['Allocation is a persistent bookkeeping task','Free-space maps, directory structures and file allocation metadata must remain coordinated. Incorrect update ordering can leave blocks leaked or multiply referenced.'],['Journaling records intent or changes','A journal helps recovery after an interruption, but different journaling modes guarantee different aspects of metadata and user-data persistence. Durable writes still require appropriate flush semantics and hardware assumptions.']],example:'If a file block is allocated but the directory operation crashes midway, a consistency checker or journal recovery needs to reconcile metadata so the allocation is not silently lost.',exercise:'Does journaling automatically replace backups?',answer:'No. Journaling supports consistency after crashes but cannot undo all application errors, deletion, corruption or catastrophic loss.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:12,pdf:583,focus:'I/O hardware and kernel I/O path',objectives:['Explain interrupt-driven I/O and DMA.','Trace synchronous versus asynchronous I/O.'],concepts:[['Controllers transfer bytes, the kernel coordinates','Device drivers submit requests to controllers; interrupts can signal completion. Direct memory access permits devices to move blocks without the CPU copying each byte.'],['Buffering and caching solve different issues','Buffering accommodates speed mismatch and burstiness; caching retains likely-to-be-reused data. Write-back caching improves throughput but creates persistence/failure concerns.']],example:'An SSD completes a read and raises an interrupt; the kernel marks the request complete and wakes a process waiting for the data, potentially triggering a scheduler decision.',exercise:'Does asynchronous I/O mean a write is durably stored as soon as the call returns?',answer:'No; completion, visibility and durability are distinct and require correct API semantics.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:13,pdf:623,focus:'Protection and access-control domains',objectives:['Separate protection mechanisms from security goals.','Explain ACLs and capabilities.'],concepts:[['Protection constrains resource use','A protection domain defines accessible objects and allowed operations. The access matrix models subjects and objects; ACLs organize rights by object while capability lists organize them by subject.'],['Least privilege limits damage','A process should receive only rights necessary for its task. Revocation, delegation and confused-deputy problems complicate apparently simple permission systems.']],example:'A report generator needs SELECT privileges on an authorized view, not a database administrator account. If compromised, its impact is constrained.',exercise:'Why can a compromised process with excessive privileges read unrelated files even when those files have no bugs?',answer:'Authorization granted the process those rights; protection cannot distinguish benign from malicious use within an overbroad privilege set.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:14,pdf:655,focus:'Threats, cryptography and auditing',objectives:['Describe malware and privilege-escalation risks.','Distinguish authentication, authorization and audit.'],concepts:[['Security includes adversarial behavior','Credential theft, vulnerable services and malicious programs can exploit both coding defects and policy mistakes. Attackers seek confidentiality, integrity or availability failures.'],['Crypto is only one layer','Encryption protects specified data in transit/at rest but cannot solve weak account authorization or malicious use of decrypted data. Logging and patching remain necessary.']],example:'A user with valid credentials can still be unauthorized to view another user’s private records. Identity verification alone does not grant the appropriate object permission.',exercise:'Why does encrypting a disk not stop an authorized session from exporting sensitive records?',answer:'The system decrypts records for authorized sessions; object-level authorization and monitoring are still needed.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:15,pdf:709,focus:'Linux architecture, processes and memory',objectives:['Describe Linux system-call and kernel abstractions.','Connect conceptual OS mechanisms to Linux-specific implementations.'],concepts:[['Linux illustrates abstract mechanisms','Processes/threads, signals, virtual memory and virtual file systems appear as practical APIs and kernel subsystems; implementation details evolve by kernel version.'],['Tools make mechanisms observable','Commands such as ps, top and /proc expose aspects of process and memory state, but observed values depend on namespaces, permissions and kernel scheduling.']],example:'A Linux process can map a file into virtual memory and page in data on demand, combining file-system and memory-management abstractions.',exercise:'Does a process’s virtual-memory size mean it occupies the same amount of physical RAM?',answer:'No. Virtual address ranges can include uncommitted, shared, file-backed and swapped mappings.',visual:'memory'}
 ],
 network:[
 {n:1,pdf:33,focus:'Internet structure and packet delay',objectives:['Trace end systems, routers and access networks.','Calculate transmission and propagation delay.'],concepts:[['The Internet is a network of networks','Hosts at the edge run applications; routers and switches forward data through access networks and core networks. Protocols standardize syntax, semantics and reaction rules.'],['Packet switching shares links','Transmission delay L/R depends on bits and bit rate; propagation delay d/s depends on distance and signal speed. Queueing adds variable delay as offered traffic approaches service capacity.'],['Layering separates responsibilities','An application sends a message through transport, network and link protocols. Encapsulation adds headers; end-to-end reliability is not provided by every layer.']],example:'A 1200-byte packet on a 12 Mbps link has transmission delay 1200×8/(12×10⁶)=0.8 ms. That is not round-trip time and ignores propagation/queueing.',exercise:'Which delay increases when the link rate halves at fixed packet size?',answer:'Transmission delay doubles; propagation depends on medium speed and physical distance.',visual:'queue'},
 {n:2,pdf:113,focus:'HTTP, DNS, sockets and application protocols',objectives:['Explain request–response and caching.','Trace DNS resolution and transport choice.'],concepts:[['Applications exchange structured messages','HTTP defines web request and response semantics; DNS maps names and other records via a distributed hierarchy. Server-side socket applications rely on transport-layer delivery properties.'],['HTTP caching is conditional','Caches reduce repeated transfers but require freshness and validation rules; a stale cached response can produce incorrect application behavior.'],['Name resolution is not data transport','A DNS lookup normally returns a resource record to aid connection establishment; subsequent application traffic follows transport and routing mechanisms separately.']],example:'A browser resolves www.example.org, establishes a transport connection (possibly QUIC for HTTP/3) and requests a page. A cached DNS answer and a cached HTTP response serve different roles.',exercise:'Why does receiving an IP address from DNS not prove the HTTPS server is authentic?',answer:'DNS address resolution is distinct from TLS authentication and certificate validation.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:3,pdf:213,focus:'UDP, TCP and congestion control',objectives:['Distinguish flow control from congestion control.','Explain ACKs, retransmission and the reliable byte stream.'],concepts:[['Transport reaches processes','Port numbers identify application endpoints, while IP routing serves host/interface delivery. UDP has minimal semantics; TCP implements ordered reliable byte delivery and connection management.'],['Reliability needs explicit state','Sequence numbers, cumulative/selective acknowledgments, timers and retransmission support reliability over losses. A delayed ACK does not by itself prove the original segment was lost.'],['Two different limits','Flow control protects the receiver’s buffer (receive window). Congestion control adapts sending to network conditions (congestion window). Actual send amount is constrained by both.']],example:'If receiver window is 12 kB and congestion window is 8 kB, the sender cannot normally have more than the smaller limit of 8 kB outstanding under a simplified byte-window model.',exercise:'If the receiver has abundant free space but a bottleneck queue is overloaded, which control is relevant?',answer:'Congestion control, not merely receiver flow control.',visual:'queue'},
 {n:4,pdf:335,focus:'Forwarding, IP packets and SDN data plane',objectives:['Separate forwarding from routing.','Explain longest-prefix matching and forwarding-table lookup.'],concepts:[['Data plane performs per-packet actions','A router parses packet headers and forwards, drops or otherwise processes traffic using configured tables. The control plane determines or installs those tables.'],['IPv4 prefixes form ranges','Longest-prefix match chooses the most specific matching routing prefix, not the route with the numerically smallest network address. Packet TTL/hop limit prevents indefinite loops.']],example:'If a router has 10.0.0.0/8 → A and 10.1.0.0/16 → B, address 10.1.2.3 matches both; the /16 route B wins.',exercise:'Does the router need to execute a full shortest-path algorithm for every packet?',answer:'No. It normally uses a forwarding table computed or configured by control-plane mechanisms.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:5,pdf:409,focus:'Routing protocols and control plane',objectives:['Contrast link-state and distance-vector routing.','Distinguish intradomain routing from BGP interdomain policy.'],concepts:[['Routing exchanges topology or reachability','Link-state algorithms build a topology view and compute paths; distance-vector approaches iteratively exchange route costs. Convergence and policy create different failure modes.'],['Internet routing is policy constrained','BGP advertises reachable prefixes between autonomous systems while applying business and safety policy. The chosen route is not necessarily the shortest path by geography or hops.']],example:'A path through a peering network may have fewer hops but be rejected by import policy, so the chosen BGP route can be longer.',exercise:'Why can an inaccurate route advertisement disrupt users outside the originating network?',answer:'Other autonomous systems may propagate and choose the advertised prefix path, redirecting or blackholing traffic.',visual:'distribution'},
 {n:6,pdf:481,focus:'Ethernet, MAC addresses and switching',objectives:['Distinguish a link-layer frame from an IP datagram.','Explain switches, ARP and channel access.'],concepts:[['The local link has its own framing','An IP datagram is encapsulated into link-layer frames at each hop; next-hop MAC addresses can change while the end-to-end IP destination remains the same.'],['Switches learn forwarding destinations','An Ethernet switch uses learned source MAC addresses to update its forwarding table. Broadcast and unknown-destination frames may be flooded within the VLAN or broadcast domain.']],example:'On a local IPv4 Ethernet link, a host may resolve a next-hop IP address to a MAC address using ARP before sending its frame.',exercise:'Does a destination MAC address identify the final Internet destination on every router hop?',answer:'No. It normally identifies a local link-layer next hop.',visual:'pipeline'},
 {n:7,pdf:563,focus:'Wireless and mobile networking',objectives:['Compare wireless contention and wired switching.','Explain mobility and cellular handover.'],concepts:[['Wireless links are shared and variable','Signal interference, fading and contention affect throughput and loss; Wi-Fi uses channel-access strategies different from traditional switched full-duplex Ethernet.'],['Mobility separates identity from attachment','Cellular systems maintain sessions as users move between radio access points, subject to handover protocols, authentication and network conditions.'],['Security starts before payload data','Authentication and key agreement establish trust/material used for protecting later wireless traffic; physical coverage is not a security boundary.']],example:'A laptop moving from one access point to another may briefly pause transmissions during reassociation even if the application keeps the same network identity.',exercise:'Why can retransmitting a Wi-Fi frame fail even when the sender has not moved?',answer:'Interference, collision and fading can corrupt the transmission or acknowledgment.',visual:'distribution'},
 {n:8,pdf:639,focus:'Network security from cryptography to TLS',objectives:['Distinguish confidentiality, integrity and endpoint authentication.','Explain the roles of TLS, VPNs, firewalls and detection.'],concepts:[['Security properties are distinct','Encryption provides confidentiality under assumptions; MACs or signatures provide integrity/authentication; PKI supports binding public keys to identities. One primitive does not automatically provide every security goal.'],['TLS secures application channels','TLS uses authenticated key exchange and symmetric authenticated encryption in a negotiated channel; its exact mechanisms depend on protocol version/configuration. TLS does not inherently protect a compromised endpoint.'],['Defense in depth crosses layers','IPsec protects network-layer traffic in configured associations, firewalls enforce traffic policy, and intrusion detection monitors patterns. Correct placement and maintenance determine effectiveness.']],example:'HTTPS can keep a password private from passive network observers, but cannot stop a phishing page or an attacker who has control of the legitimate web server.',exercise:'If a TLS session is authenticated but the application leaks private data in a public URL, is TLS enough?',answer:'No. Transport security cannot correct application-layer data exposure and authorization defects.',visual:'pipeline'}
 ]
});
